 Once upon a time, in the lush jungles of Stranglethorn Vale, a gnome warlock named Pixel ventured with his succubus companion, Siren. Pixel, a cunning and ambitious gnome, had chosen Siren for her damage output and utility. She could seduce enemies, allowing Pixel to safely attack from range, and her lash of pain inflicted significant shadow damage.

One day, while battling trolls, Pixel realized that Siren's abilities, while powerful, were not optimized for every situation. He missed the Voidwalker's tanking capabilities, especially during moments when he needed to focus on avoiding damage or dispatching multiple enemies simultaneously.

The riddle you presented is similar to Pixel's situation. While Succubi offer better damage and utility for leveling and questing, Voidwalkers excel at threat generation and crowd control. Warlocks often prefer Voidwalkers when they need to tank multiple enemies or maintain aggro on a single target, even if it means sacrificing some damage output.

In summary, most warlocks are seen with Voidwalkers not because Succubi are inferior, but because Voidwalkers provide unique and valuable utility for specific situations during gameplay. [end of text]


